                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T10:03:51.877577794Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @blocker
                        text "**Attempted pin bump cfb16cee → 72d796f1 (upstream head, 2026-08-15): reverted on a byte-parity regression.** Upstream moved ~30 commits (renderer final-position anchoring, compiled-terminfo decoding, signal-slot lifetime, secure temp files, non-UTF-8 history paths, key-sequence deadlines). Two API breaks were adapted cleanly and are preserved as a ready patch in the session scratchpad (`nshedit-72d796f-bump.patch`): `ReadEffect::KeySequence` gained `{ deadline: ReadDeadline }` — host now waits `deadline.remaining()` via `nshedit_plat::terminal::wait_for_input`, matching the reference driver — and `HistoryMatch` gained `LiteralOrRegex`, which this host resolves to the shell's pattern matcher alongside `Contains` (consumer policy: nsh history search speaks shell pattern notation, the dash/libedit contract ptydiff pins; the editor's literal-then-regex rule would also require a `regex` dependency the crate forbids).\n\n**The blocker is upstream:** at 72d796f1, with the editor engaged (-E/-V), every prompt render is wrapped in `\\r \\x1b7 \\x1b8 <prompt> \\x1b[K <echo> \\x1b8` — DECSC/DECRC \"owned terminal region\" anchoring (upstream 98b96dd/661fc95) emitted unconditionally in the inline profile. libedit emits none of that for plain sequential input; ptydiff fails 11/43 (every -E/-V case incl. both ^C editor cases; all non-editor cases pass; adaptation-touched search paths pass). Raw-byte evidence, port vs ref: `b'\\r\\x1b7\\x1b8$ \\x1b[Kecho X\\x1b8\\r\\nX\\r\\n'` vs `b'$ echo X\\r\\nX\\r\\n'` (captured via ptydiff's own run() with -E). Same class as the pre-cfb16cee absolute-cursor-redraw bug upstream fixed: inline/plain-profile rendering must stay byte-parity with libedit for sequential input. Pin stays at cfb16cee until an upstream fix lands; re-apply the scratchpad patch when re-pinning. Note ptydiff's ANSI-strip regex does not cover bare ESC7/ESC8 — which is why this was caught; if it ever \"passes\" after an upstream fix, confirm the sequences are gone rather than newly stripped."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T11:53:01.913315271Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @unblock
                        text "Pin-bump blocker resolved. Upstream fix `8059f66` (\"Own a single-line render region without emitting anchor bytes\") lands the lazy region claim: single-row frames own an Inline region at zero bytes, multi-row anchoring byte-identical, contract pinned upstream by a test asserting `$ echo X\\n` with no ESC byte. Consumer repinned in `39f2bce` with the two API adaptations applied; gates on the repinned binary `8142664c`: cargo 6/6 suites, divtest 44/0, ptydiff 43/0 FLAKY=0, POSIX 660/51 with the failing set exactly the 51-name core (zero extras — even the flaky fringe passed this run). The scratchpad patch is consumed; remaining node scope is unchanged (distribution/publishing)."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id output-is-a-writer
                title "output.rs becomes a writer the shell holds, not four statics on descriptors"
                priority 0
                kind @refactor
                deps (delete-memalloc)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-10T00:04:37.702624978Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-11T20:00:23.265203268Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @blocker
                        text "The writer landed and the libc formatting bridge is gone (552f2b1, 5a259a4, 8d68cc0). One decision is outstanding before this can go Done: how `printf %a`/`%A` is rendered. Rust std has no hexadecimal float formatting, so the port currently prints an exactly round-tripping decimal instead of C's hex form. Measured cost: 576 differential cases fail — 1 in aud_bltin_printf, 181 in printffuzz, 394 in printffuzz2 — and every one of them contains an %a or %A conversion. Nothing else diverges in 12264 printf cases; printf2 and printffuzz3 are 113/0 and 4000/0, ptydiff 37/0, exec/foundation clean, divtest 44/0. Sanctioning it in the register was investigated and will not work honestly: a precision-bearing %a rounds the value (`%.0a` of 1.9 is 0x2p+0), so an entry cannot even assert \"the same number written differently\", and anything looser would be a blind spot over 576 fuzz cases contrary to the register's own rule. The two ways out are (a) render %a by transcribing the IEEE-754 fields with `format!(\"{:x}\")` — no digit generation or rounding table, but it is a bespoke %a renderer, which the architecture directive forbids — or (b) accept the decimal form and the 576-case hole. Isolated in `Spec::hexadecimal` in crates/nsh/src/bltin/printf/conv.rs so either is a local change."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-11T20:55:22.508593491Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @unblock
                        text "Resolved, and not by either option the blocker offered. Brendan rejected both: no bespoke %a renderer and no 576-case register hole, because the question itself came from keeping a runtime %-conversion interpreter in the crate. It is gone — the printf builtin with it — under [dec:nsh:no-format-interpreters]. printf resolves through PATH like any other utility. Output stays what this node made it: Output is an io::Write the shell owns, formatting happens through write! at typed call sites, and nothing in the crate now reads a format pattern at runtime. Final gates: runall 47826/0 FLAKY=28 XFAIL=11 with no stale register entries, POSIX cases 661/50 against the C's 655/56, ptydiff 37/0, divtest 58/0, cargo test 62 lib + 11 integration."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-11T20:55:43.687147342Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
