            {
                id public-api
                title "Implement the public API and close the surface: 35 pub mod down to what an embedder needs"
                priority 0
                kind @feat
                deps (no-ambient-state host-owns-signals public-api-design)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-14T15:47:48.64986797Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "**Obligation transferred from `move-state`: the shell's streams cannot move onto the instance until this node gives `Shell` a public constructor. The evidence is a revert, not an argument.**\n\n`move-state` surveyed `streams.rs:98 STREAMS` as one of the easiest tables in the crate — one static, `Copy` payload, two functions, one writer at `main_fn` which is the constructor site, seventeen mechanical read sites — attempted the move, and reverted it. The blocker is not the read sites and is not effort. It is that **`set` has two other callers that have no shell and cannot be given one**:\n\n* `streams::install(s) -> Borrowed` is the *embedding* surface. Its only in-crate callers are five integration tests under `crates/nsh/tests/` — `streams_embed.rs`, `errors_are_values.rs`, `glob_buffer.rs`, `expansion_buffer.rs` — which stand in for a frontend pointing the shell's I/O somewhere before running it.\n* `Borrowed::restore(self)` puts the host's descriptors back. Giving it a receiver means the drop guard carries one, which is a lifetime on a type whose whole job is to be droppable.\n\n**The decisive fact: an integration test cannot construct a `Shell`.** `context::Shell::new` is `pub(crate)` on purpose — `move-state` deleted `Shell::detached()` precisely to make \"every instance is the shell\" an invariant the type enforces rather than a convention. So the moment `streams::set` takes a receiver, five integration tests stop compiling, and there is no in-crate fix that does not make the constructor public — which would undo the invariant that node exists to establish.\n\nThat is not a test-design accident. It is the real question, and it is this node's: **does the shell's I/O belong to the process or to the instance?** `docs/api-design.md` answers it here — §7 makes `Streams` a builder input and §2 makes `Shell` publicly constructible through the builder. Once `Shell::builder().streams(..)` exists, `STREAMS` moves as a field initialiser and `install`/`restore` become the builder's business with no `set` at all.\n\n**What this node owes back.** When the builder lands, say so on `move-state`'s log (or move the field here directly, if that is cheaper once the constructor is public) — `move-state` has struck `streams` from its own table order and named this node as the dependency, so nothing will pick it up otherwise. `thread-context`'s close-out already recorded that `Streams::install` and the builder are this node's and are not to be touched by `move-state`; this is the same line, found from the other side and now with a compile failure behind it.\n\n**Also relevant when the surface closes:** `move-state` found that `docs/api-design.md` §5's baseline is stale in three ways that affect §5's field table this node will implement — `crates/nsh/src/gen/` does not exist (so every \"excluding `gen/`\" qualifier is a no-op and the real count is 107 `static mut`, not 124), five statics §5 lists are already gone to upstream nodes, and `output.rs`'s `output`/`errout`/`preverrout`/`out1`/`out2` were collapsed into a single `SHELL_IO` by `output-is-a-writer`, so the `io: ShellIo` row describes a module that no longer looks like that."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id sorted-tables
                title "env and alias print sorted: BTreeMap tables, and the register's first entry"
                priority 0
                kind @fix
                deps (sanctioned-divergences)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T11:44:42.574884968Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T11:57:04.358106034Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The title named four builtins and two of them were wrong. `export -p` and a bare `set` both print through `showvars`, which already `qsort`s with `vpcmp` before printing, so both shells printed those sorted before this change and print them sorted after it. Only `env` -- anything that reads `environ`, since `listvars` builds `execve`'s `envp` -- and a bare `alias` change. Measured on both binaries before touching anything. The register entry is scoped to `env`, `printenv` and `alias` for the same reason: naming `export -p` or `set` would let it excuse a permutation that could only be a regression.\n\nThe corpus observes it once, not thirty times: `aud_state_var.txt`'s `export XV=1 YV=2; echo \"$XV$YV\"; env | grep -E '^(XV|YV)='`. The forty-odd `env` cases in `aud_state_gen3` are all `env | grep -c '^NAME='`, which counts rather than lists, and the `alias` cases print one alias or none.\n\nFive C symbols lost their target counterpart -- `hashval`, `hashvar`, `varcmp`, `varequal`, `vpcmp` -- since all five existed only to serve the hash table. Retired with the transaction `delete-gen` established: marker lines out of `docs/spec/port/src/var.md` with every word of prose kept, entries out of `plan/annotations.styx` and `plan/.port-manifest.styx`. Spec coverage 460/460 to 455/455."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T16:24:49.364256759Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Done. Both halves landed and both are exercised on every sweep.\n\nThe tables: `var.rs` and `alias.rs` hold `BTreeMap`s, so `env`, `printenv` and `alias` print in name order where dash prints in the order its 39 hash buckets happen to chain. `export -p` and `set` are deliberately untouched — dash already `qsort`s those in `showvars` (`src/var.c:409`), so both shells print them sorted and always did.\n\nThe register: `dsdiv_sorted_tables` in `tests/harness/divergences.sh` is the first entry, and it is the reason the sweep can still report FAIL=0. It asserts five things rather than ignoring the difference — matching exit status, a case that actually runs `env`/`printenv`/`alias`, an identical multiset of lines, only assignment-shaped lines having moved, and the port's blocks being the sorted side. The last two are what keep it from excusing a regression: `ds_same_lines` alone would excuse every permutation, including one that is neither shell's.\n\nCurrent state on master: `runall.sh 8` reports XFAIL=1 with no STALE (the entry fires in `aud_state_var`), and `divtest.sh` reports PASS=28 FAIL=0 — twenty-eight tests that all assert *refusals*, because the way this entry fails is by matching too much.\n\nKnown limit, pinned by `divtest.sh` so it cannot drift silently: `ds_blocks_sorted` reads each maximal run of assignment-shaped lines as one block, so a case printing two environments back to back with nothing between them would be refused and reported as a failure. Nothing in `tests/corpus` does, and a loud refusal is the right way for this entry to be wrong."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T16:24:56.014094132Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id edition-2024
                title "Move the workspace to edition 2024, and take unsafe-op count as the real minimal-unsafe metric"
                priority 0
                kind @build
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T19:30:34.867446656Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Merged to master at af1434d and fully gated there.\n\n```\nrunall.sh 6   ==== TOTAL PASS=61498 FAIL=0 FLAKY=50 XFAIL=1 ====   no STALE\nptydiff       INTERACTIVE (pty, sandboxed): PASS=31 FAIL=0 FLAKY=0\nPOSIX         cases: PASS=657 FAIL=54    reference: PASS=655 FAIL=56\n```\n\nThe sweep is what this node needed rather than a formality. Edition 2024 changes the temporary scope of tail expressions and of `if let` scrutinees, which moves when temporaries drop; `docs/errors-are-values.md` establishes that the crate defines exactly one `impl Drop` and it is `#[cfg(test)]`, so the exposure was always to timing rather than to observable destructors — but \"always\" was an argument and this is a measurement. 61,498 differential cases, 31 interactive pty cases and 657 POSIX cases cannot tell the two editions apart.\n\nFLAKY=50 is contention: three agents were running their own sweeps on the same box. It is not a signal; the flakiness classifier re-runs a divergent case eleven times and counts it flaky only when the reference disagrees with itself.\n\nRecorded for whoever turns the lint on: the 5,010 `unsafe_op_in_unsafe_fn` sites are 2,747 unsafe calls, 1,221 raw-pointer dereferences, 1,018 mutable-static reads and 24 union-field accesses. The three sources map onto three open nodes — the dereferences to [[delete-memalloc]] and [[delete-libc-calls]], the static reads to [[thread-context]] and [[move-state]], the unsafe calls to whatever remains after both. When the figure is small enough to wrap by hand, deleting `#![allow(unsafe_op_in_unsafe_fn)]` from `lib.rs` re-measures it."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T19:30:39.321605436Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

